<p>&nbsp;<br>&nbsp;<br>
<table align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="80%">
			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB"><font size="3" class="MainBReverse">&nbsp;<b>T</b>&nbsp;</font>he Armigers of Mirth is a cross-virtuality guild dedicated to the exploration
				of taverns, the pursuit fair play, and generally having a good time.
				</td>
			</tr>
			
			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB" align="center"><img src="images/arm_spear2.gif" WIDTH="500" HEIGHT="21"></td>
			</tr>	
			
			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB"><font size="3" class="MainBReverse">&nbsp;<b>W</b>&nbsp;</font>henever possible, players are required to carry a minimum of two drinks with
				them at all times.  Drinking is a great way to solve in-game problems. 
				Remember, when in doubt about how to resolve a situation, offer the other
				player a drink.
				</td>
			</tr>	
			
			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB" align="center"><img src="images/arm_spear2r.gif" WIDTH="500" HEIGHT="21"></td>
			</tr>	
			
			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB"><font size="3" class="MainBReverse">&nbsp;<b>I</b>&nbsp;</font>n virtualities which support the skill, Armigers are required to take up
				brewing.  You need not progress beyond hobbyist levels in the skill, but
				becoming a master brewer is a prized goal among the Armigers.  A Player that
				becomes a Master Brewer will be awarded a personalized shot glass acknowledging
				their illustrious achievement (okay its a virtual shotglass).
				</td>
			</tr>

			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB" align="center"><img src="images/arm_spear2.gif" WIDTH="500" HEIGHT="21"></td>
			</tr>	

			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB"><font size="3" class="MainBReverse">&nbsp;<b>N</b>&nbsp;</font>ever drink alone, unless you're celebrating with a Guild Member or Members
				located too far away for you to reasonably gather together.  Always bring
				enough for everyone when you come to drink.</td>
			</tr>

			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB" align="center"><img src="images/arm_spear2r.gif" WIDTH="500" HEIGHT="21"></td>
			</tr>	

			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB"><font size="3" class="MainBReverse">&nbsp;<b>W</b>&nbsp;</font>hile we don't require role-playing at all times, we believe that it adds fun
				and flavor to any game and certainly recommend role-playing as much as possible.</td>
			</tr>

			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB" align="center"><img src="images/arm_spear2.gif" WIDTH="500" HEIGHT="21"></td>
			</tr>

			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB"><font size="3" class="MainBReverse">&nbsp;<b>K</b>&nbsp;</font>ill stealing, in virtualities where such a concept applies, will not be
				tolerated.  Your actions reflect on The Guild as a whole, and therefore you
				must act in a manner that won't adversely affect our image and reputation. When
				in doubt, offer the other player a drink.
				</td>
			</tr>

				<tr>
				<td Class="MainB" align="center"><img src="images/arm_spear2r.gif" WIDTH="500" HEIGHT="21"></td>
			</tr>	

			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB"><font size="3" class="MainBReverse">&nbsp;<b>B</b>&nbsp;</font>efore selling any major item check with the guild members that are online
				first, another guild member may need or want it.  Guild members should get
				first crack at any such items.  This does not mean you have to give your stuff
				away for free.  We simply ask that you allow fellow Armigers the opportunity to
				pay a fair price for an item before you test the open market.  A drink should
				accompany every trade and transaction (again, assuming the given virtuality
				supports this action) so that a toast may be made to its successful completion.</font>
				</td>
			</tr>

			<tr>
				<td Class="MainB" align="center"><img src="images/arm_spear2.gif" WIDTH="500" HEIGHT="21"></td>
			</tr>
		
